#What is Agile methodology?

Agile is a software development methodology/approach in which software is developed and delivered incrementally in small iterations, with continuous customer feedback, collaboration, and the ability to adapt to changing requirements.

In simple terms:

Agile means we don't build the entire product first and test it at the end. We develop, test, get feedback, and improve the product continuously in small iterations.

Example

Suppose we are building a real-estate application.

Instead of:

Requirement → Development of entire application → Testing → Release

Agile works more like:

Sprint 1 → Develop + Test → Demo/Feedback → Sprint 2 → Develop + Test → Demo/Feedback → ...

For example:

Sprint	Feature
Sprint 1	Login/Signup
Sprint 2	Property Search
Sprint 3	Property Filters
Sprint 4	Property Details
Sprint 5	Favorites

Key characteristics of Agile

Iterative & incremental development
Product is built in small increments.
Continuous testing
QA is involved throughout the development cycle, not only after development is complete.
Customer collaboration
Customer/stakeholder feedback is collected frequently.
Responding to change
Requirements can be changed or reprioritized based on business needs.
Cross-functional team
Developers, QA, Product Owner, Scrum Master, etc. collaborate closely.
Frequent delivery
Working software is delivered regularly.



